Shark Game — CS50x Week 0

Overview

Shark Game is an arcade-style game developed in Scratch as my Week 0 project for Harvard University's CS50x course.

The player controls a shark and must catch moving fish to increase their score. The goal is to reach 50 points before the countdown timer reaches zero.

How to Play

- Start the game using the green flag.
- Control the shark and move it toward the fish.
- Catching a fish increases the score.
- Fish disappear after being caught and return to the game at a new position.
- Reach 50 points before the timer reaches zero to win.
- If the timer reaches zero before reaching 50 points, the player loses.

Features

- Player-controlled shark
- Multiple moving fish
- Collision detection
- Score system
- Countdown timer
- Win and Game Over screens
- Sound effects and background music
- Random fish positioning
- Broadcast messages between sprites
- Custom Scratch block with an input parameter

Programming Concepts

This project applies several fundamental programming concepts introduced in CS50x Week 0, including:

- Variables for managing the score and timer
- Conditional statements for game logic
- Loops for continuously running game behavior
- Events and broadcasts for communication between sprites
- Randomization for changing fish positions
- Collision detection
- Custom blocks and inputs for abstraction

Custom Block

The project includes a custom block called "Move Fish (Speed)".

The block accepts a "Speed" input and uses it to control the movement of a fish. This demonstrates how repeated behavior can be abstracted into a reusable custom block rather than placing all movement instructions directly in the main game script.

Goal of the Project

The purpose of this project was to apply the programming concepts learned in CS50x Week 0 by creating a complete interactive game from scratch.

Rather than simply creating a visual animation, the project combines variables, conditions, loops, events, broadcasts, sounds, collision detection, and abstraction into a single playable program.

Project

